[86.283582, "o", "  qwen3.6-35b — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: find /usr/local/lib/python3.13 -name \"*.whl\" 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash find /usr/local/lib/python3.13 -name \"*.whl\" 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 14 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: find /usr/local/lib/python3.13 -name \"ensurepip\" -type d 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash find /usr/local/lib/python3.13 -name \"ensurepip\" -type d 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ There's a bundled pip wheel. The issue is that `ensurepip` sees the dist-…\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m ensurepip --default-pip --upgrade --force 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m ensurepip --default-pip --upgrade --force 2>&1\r\n    ✗ bash — exit 2\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ No `--force` flag. Let me remove the stale dist-info and try again:\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -"]
[86.283707, "o", "rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Now let me verify:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 18 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 19 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Let me do a quick smoke test to confirm package installation works:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 20 tool call(s) so far\r\nEverything is working now.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong:** Your `pip` installation was corrupted — the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata folder existed in `site-packages`, but the actual `pip/` package directory (the Python code) was missing. This made `pip3` crash with `ModuleNotFoundErro"]
[86.283738, "o", "r: No module named 'pip'` because the wrapper script could find its entry point but there was nothing to import.\r\n\r\n**What I did:** Removed the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` directory, then ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` which extracted pip from the bundled wheel (`/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`). Pip is now fully functional.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=20 surface=1595a1d14f70\r\n"]
